<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Collectivization in the Soviet Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Collectivisation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Collectivization was the Soviet policy, carried out between 1928 and 1940 as part of the first five-year plan, of merging individual peasant holdings and labour into collective and state farms, the kolkhozes and sovkhozes, to secure grain for the cities, industry and export. Joining was not voluntary: from about 1% of farmland in 1928, the share rose to about 90% of agriculture by 1936. Peasants resisted with protests and armed revolt and slaughtered their livestock. Stalin resolved to eliminate the kulaks as a class by dispossession, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> and execution, and the famine of 1932–33, in which an estimated 5.7 to 8.7 million people starved, followed the collapse of production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/collectivization-in-the-soviet-union.jpg" alt="Collectivization in the Soviet Union" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:“Strengthen_working_discipline_in_collective_farms”_–_Uzbek,_Tashkent,_1933_(Mardjani).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> the policy of prodrazvyorstka obliged peasants to surrender their surplus produce at a fixed price. After the war the New Economic Policy replaced it with a less drastic food tax. From 1927 a crisis in grain deliveries developed, which grew more serious as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> pressed ahead with industrialisation and needed more food for its cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> and the Opposition had advocated industrialisation with voluntary agricultural cooperatives and collective farms. Some scholars hold that Stalin took over the Left Opposition's position, while others argue that Trotsky's programme differed from the forced collectivisation that Stalin carried out after 1928, because of the brutality of its enforcement.</p><p>In 1928 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> fell 2 million tons short in the grain it bought from neighbouring markets. Stalin claimed that the grain had been produced but was being hoarded by kulaks, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>, instead of raising the price, ordered the requisition of 2.5 million tons. Much of it was taken from middle peasants, and resistance spread in 1929, with violent incidents and hoarding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Between September and December 1929 the share of collectivised households rose from 7.4 per cent to 15 per cent, and in the first two months of 1930 eleven million households joined, taking the total to nearly 60 per cent. The Party sent 25,000 industrial workers, the twenty-five-thousanders, to the countryside to help. On 2 March 1930 Pravda published Stalin's article Dizzy with Success, which called for a pause and blamed overzealous officials. Peasants began to leave the farms, but pressure resumed, and decrees of March 1931 forbade peasants to leave the collective farms. By 1936 about 90 per cent of Soviet agriculture was collectivised.</p><p>Peasants resisted with protests, armed revolt and the slaughter of livestock. Many saw the kolkhoz as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serfdom">second serfdom</a>, since entry was forced, leaving needed permission and the state took most of the crop at low prices. The closing of churches and the arrest of priests gave further cause for revolt, and in 1929 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> put down a revolt in Buryatia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The immediate effect was often a fall in output and the halving of livestock herds. The number of horses fell from 36.1 million in 1928 to 21.0 million in 1941. In Kazakhstan livestock fell sharply, 1.5 million people fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and as many as a million of those who stayed died in famine.</p><p>Most historians agree that the disruption and the peasants' resistance contributed greatly to the great famine of 1932–1933, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, where it is called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a>. Information about the disaster was suppressed, and Ukrainian villages were blacklisted and penalised for supposed sabotage. Only in the late 1950s did Soviet livestock stocks approach their 1928 levels.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Collectivization in the Soviet Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
